# glib-networking -- the TLS backend behind every GLib/GIO network connection,
# and the package that makes libsoup able to speak HTTPS at all.
#
# libsoup provides NO TLS OF ITS OWN. It reaches TLS through glib's GTlsBackend,
# which this package implements. libsoup's meson makes that explicit rather than
# leaving it to be discovered:
#     assert(rres.compiled() and rres.returncode() == 0,
#            'libsoup requires glib-networking for TLS support')
# with tls_check defaulting to true. Passing -Dtls_check=false would produce a
# libsoup that builds, installs, links and CANNOT SPEAK HTTPS, in a stack whose
# whole purpose is fetching from Flathub over HTTPS.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/glib-networking/2.80/glib-networking-2.80.1.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=b80e2874157cd55071f1b6710fa0b911d5ac5de106a9ee2a4c9c7bee61782f8e
SRC_DIR=glib-networking-2.80.1

# ==============================================================================
# THIS RECIPE DEPARTS FROM AN EXPLICIT UPSTREAM INSTRUCTION. READ THE WHOLE
# ARGUMENT BEFORE CHANGING IT.
# ==============================================================================
#
# meson_options.txt says, verbatim:
#
#     # The OpenSSL backend is provided for systems where licensing
#     # considerations prohibit use of certain dependencies of GnuTLS.
#     # General-purpose Linux distros should leave it disabled. Please don't
#     # second-guess our defaults.
#     option('gnutls',  type: 'feature', value: 'enabled')
#     option('openssl', type: 'feature', value: 'disabled')
#
# This recipe enables openssl and disables gnutls anyway. The justification is
# NOT that the gnutls chain is expensive -- it is four to six recipes, which is
# a bounded and boring cost next to the twenty-three already written.
#
# THE REASON IS THAT gnome-software WOULD OTHERWISE CARRY TWO TLS STACKS IN ONE
# PROCESS. It links libflatpak, which links libcurl, which links libcrypto. It
# ALSO links libsoup, which reaches TLS through this package. Choosing gnutls
# here does not replace openssl in that process; it ADDS to it -- two TLS
# implementations, two certificate verification paths and two trust-store
# configurations, in the program that installs software.
#
# That is the same argument ostree's --with-curl was decided on, and it is the
# worse instance of the two: two HTTP clients disagreeing produces a confusing
# failure, whereas TWO VERIFICATION PATHS DISAGREEING DOES NOT FAIL AT ALL -- one
# of them accepts something the other would have rejected, and nothing reports
# it.
#
# UPSTREAM'S STATED REASON DOES NOT APPLY HERE. They say the openssl backend
# exists for systems where LICENSING prohibits GnuTLS dependencies. Duct has no
# such constraint. Departing from advice whose premise does not hold, with a
# reason stated, is a different act from ignoring the advice -- but only because
# the reason is written here.
#
# THE WARNING IS STILL REAL EVEN THOUGH ITS PREMISE IS NOT. The openssl backend
# is the less exercised path, and a weak verifier's failure mode is that
# EVERYTHING WORKS. So this decision is conditional on a demonstration that it
# actually verifies, with a negative arm: a valid endpoint must SUCCEED and an
# untrusted or mismatched certificate must FAIL. The positive arm alone proves
# only that bytes moved. See VERIFICATION-REQUIRED below.
#
# REVISIT CONDITION: if gnutls is ever packaged for another reason, or if this
# backend is found lacking, this decision flips -- disable openssl, enable
# gnutls, and the two-stacks argument disappears because gnutls becomes the only
# one. Same shape as flatpak's system_helper waiting on polkit.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PROXY OPTIONS, which are not cosmetic
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# -Dlibproxy=disabled. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS ENABLED and libproxy is not packaged.
# -Dgnome_proxy=enabled. Already the default; gsettings-desktop-schemas IS
#   packaged, so this resolves correctly -- named so it stays that way.
# -Denvironment_proxy=enabled. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS DISABLED, and this is the one
#   that matters. With libproxy absent and this off, http_proxy and https_proxy
#   in the environment ARE IGNORED ENTIRELY: a proxied network silently has no
#   proxy, and the resulting connectivity failure looks like a broken mirror
#   rather than a missing feature.
#
# -Dinstalled_tests=false is already the upstream default here; recorded rather
# than passed, for the same reason as xdg-dbus-proxy's.
MESON_ARGS="-Dgnutls=disabled -Dopenssl=enabled \
-Dlibproxy=disabled -Dgnome_proxy=enabled -Denvironment_proxy=enabled"
